WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.334
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:02.334 --> 00:00:04.337
is an Emmy and Golden Globe
winning actress.

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:06.606
You know her work in the
critically acclaimed series

00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:08.207
"The Marvelous Mrs. Maisel."

00:00:08.207 --> 00:00:09.342
She stars in...

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:13.713
...which is playing at the
BAM Harvey Theater in Brooklyn.

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:15.381
Please welcome back to the show
our friend

00:00:15.381 --> 00:00:16.682
Rachel Brosnahan, everybody.

00:00:16.682 --> 00:00:18.284
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:21.387
[ Band plays mellow tune ]

00:00:21.387 --> 00:00:34.333 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:35.901
Welcome back to the show!

00:00:35.901 --> 00:00:37.369
-Thank you.
It's nice to be back.

00:00:37.369 --> 00:00:39.905
-I'm so happy to have you.
It is very --

00:00:39.905 --> 00:00:41.574
I don't know if it's as funny
to you as it is to me.

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:44.010
You finished wrapping
"Mrs. Maisel."

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.478
Obviously, you've done
this incredible show

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:46.745
for five seasons.

00:00:46.745 --> 00:00:49.282
It takes place in New York City
in the 1960s.

00:00:49.282 --> 00:00:51.784
And now you've moved
on from that

00:00:51.784 --> 00:00:53.719
to do this wonderful play

00:00:53.719 --> 00:00:57.156
where you play a woman
in 1960s New York City again.

00:00:57.156 --> 00:00:58.190
[ Laughter ]
-Yes.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:00:59.058
-So this is kind of
your move now.

00:00:59.058 --> 00:01:00.960
-I guess it is.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.729
Maybe next, I'll get to go
to the '70s.

00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:04.430
That would be really exciting.
-Oh, my God, yeah.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:05.831
We would love to see you
in the '70s.

00:01:05.831 --> 00:01:07.533
[ Laughter ]
-Thank you so much.
Or the '90s.

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:09.769
That's vintage now.
Take me to the '90s.

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:11.136
-Don't tell me
that the '90s is vintage.

00:01:11.136 --> 00:01:12.405
-Isn't that terrifying?
-That breaks my heart, yeah.

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:14.407
[ Laughter ]
-Me too.

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:16.742
Yeah, no. I mean, what's
interesting about this is

00:01:16.742 --> 00:01:18.644
I feel like, you know,
obviously, it's a coincidence.

00:01:18.644 --> 00:01:20.580
I feel like I'm searching
for great parts

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.181
and great writing
and great scripts,

00:01:22.181 --> 00:01:24.416
but, um --

00:01:24.416 --> 00:01:26.752
I feel like Iris, who I play in

00:01:26.752 --> 00:01:27.954
"The Sign in Sidney Brustein's
Window,"

00:01:27.954 --> 00:01:30.022
would be one of the beatniks

00:01:30.022 --> 00:01:32.725
in the comedy club
of one of Midge's shows.

00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:34.260
-Yes.
-They're pretty different gals.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.162
-Exactly.
It turns out there were a lot

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:38.264
of different kinds of people
in New York City in the '60s.

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:39.933
[ Laughter ]
-It turns out not all women
were the same.

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:41.767
-Not all women were the same.
-Yeah.

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:43.670
Despite men's best efforts
in the '60s.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:45.238
[ Laughter ]
-That's exactly right.
-Yeah.

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:47.873
They managed to have
a breadth of experiences.

00:01:47.873 --> 00:01:50.944
So, this is the same playwright
who wrote "Raisin in the Sun"

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:54.447
-Yeah.
-and this is her final play
and it's lesser known.

00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:57.383
Is it cool to sort of bring back
a very famous playwright's

00:01:57.383 --> 00:02:00.019
sort of -- you know,
I wouldn't say secondary work,

00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:01.520
but certainly lesser known work.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:02.555
Is it cool to bring it out

00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:03.790
to an audience
that hasn't seen it?

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:06.592
-Yeah, it's been really,
really special.

00:02:06.592 --> 00:02:10.095
Lorraine Hansberry
was such a gifted writer.

00:02:10.095 --> 00:02:12.565
So many people know her work
from "A Raisin in the Sun,"

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:13.766
but this is a really
different play

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:15.435
about a really different group
of people

00:02:15.435 --> 00:02:18.805
and it's a piece that is
so much lesser known.

00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:21.206
It's been really, really special
to get to bring it to life

00:02:21.206 --> 00:02:23.609
with this cast.
Our director, Annie Kauffman,

00:02:23.609 --> 00:02:26.512
did the play back in 2016
in Chicago

00:02:26.512 --> 00:02:28.314
and it's just been the best.

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.350
It's been so awesome to hear
from so many people

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.919
who were familiar
with her work from "Raisin"

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:34.787
about how this play
has moved them,

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:36.489
and also people who didn't know
her work at all

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:38.324
and this being
their first introduction

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:41.427
to the force that was
Lorraine Hansberry.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:44.464
-You mentioned the cast.
Your costar Oscar Isaacs,

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:47.567
one of our favorites here
as well.

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:50.069
Oscar Isaac -- excuse me.
And how is it working with him?

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:51.503
Were you familiar with his work?

00:02:51.503 --> 00:02:54.474
-[ Laughs ]
Yes, yes.

00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:58.911
[ Laughter ]
No. Oscar's the best.
We've had so much fun.

00:02:58.911 --> 00:03:02.649
I had, you know, I was familiar
with quite a bit of his work,

00:03:02.649 --> 00:03:04.283
but I did bust myself
the other day.

00:03:04.283 --> 00:03:06.885
He was messing around,
doing a doing a British accent

00:03:06.885 --> 00:03:08.187
during rehearsals
and I was like,

00:03:08.187 --> 00:03:11.257
"Oh, you know, your British
accent's pretty good.

00:03:11.257 --> 00:03:12.858
Where did you learn that?"
And he was like,

00:03:12.858 --> 00:03:14.193
"I -- I just --

00:03:14.193 --> 00:03:16.129
you know, I just did it
in 'Moon Knight.'"

00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:19.232
I was like,
"Which I have definitely seen

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:20.800
[ Laughter ]
and enjoyed very much."

00:03:22.868 --> 00:03:24.737
Yeah. I keep getting busted --

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:25.938
-"You were so good in that,

00:03:25.938 --> 00:03:27.373
I thought it was someone else."

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:29.308
[ Laughter ]
-"I didn't even recognize you.

00:03:29.308 --> 00:03:31.276
I thought that was
a British man."

00:03:31.276 --> 00:03:32.244
-"That was you!

00:03:32.244 --> 00:03:35.181
That was you."
-"It was you?!"

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:36.515
[ Laughter ]

00:03:36.515 --> 00:03:38.851
-You also have a very talented
young actor in the show,

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:41.387
Julian De Niro,
who is Robert De Niro's son.

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:43.155
-Yes.
-Does that mean
that Robert De Niro

00:03:43.155 --> 00:03:44.624
has come to see the show?

00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:46.758
-He has.
He's been an amazing dad.

00:03:46.758 --> 00:03:50.363
He's come to see the show twice
and he's so lovely.

00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:52.732
Julian is
extraordinarily talented

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:55.801
and has just been fantastic
to work with.

00:03:55.801 --> 00:03:58.037
But he did come
to our first preview,

00:03:58.037 --> 00:04:01.741
which is an amazing like
A++ dad move.

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:04.643
So mean, as an actor.
-Yeah. His first preview.

00:04:04.643 --> 00:04:06.412
[ Laughter ]
-Actor to actor --
-You definitely want people

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:08.815
to come at least on night three.
-That's what I'm saying.

00:04:08.815 --> 00:04:12.117
-Yeah.
-But he was very, very lovely.

00:04:12.117 --> 00:04:14.387
-When did you --
Were you doing school plays

00:04:14.387 --> 00:04:16.455
when you were young?
Is that how you got started?

00:04:16.455 --> 00:04:18.323
-Yeah, I did, you know,
I did some

00:04:18.323 --> 00:04:19.758
kindergarten tree work

00:04:19.758 --> 00:04:21.060
[ Laughter ]
that was --
-Oh, look at you.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.962
that was highly excellent...
-You still hear about it.

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:25.397
-...if I do say so myself.
-Yeah.

00:04:25.397 --> 00:04:27.099
-Yeah, I started, I started,
you know,

00:04:27.099 --> 00:04:30.103
doing -- diddling around in --
That has a --

00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:32.371
[ Laughter ]

00:04:32.371 --> 00:04:35.340
Just go back right now.
-That's like a stage term.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:36.709
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:39.011
Doing some diddling --
-To diddle is to sort of, yeah.

00:04:39.011 --> 00:04:41.046
-Thank you so much.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:41.046 --> 00:04:44.683
In elementary school,
junior high, and high school.

00:04:44.683 --> 00:04:47.886
My high school had this really
cool program called Stunts

00:04:47.886 --> 00:04:50.589
where the students
put on their own show

00:04:50.589 --> 00:04:55.027
and kind of rewrite the words
to well-known music and songs

00:04:55.027 --> 00:04:57.629
and, as you can expect, it's --

00:04:57.629 --> 00:04:59.599
-So there's no sort of --

00:04:59.599 --> 00:05:01.400
no overseeing adult?
-There's not a lot of adult

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:02.434
oversight, yeah.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:05:02.434 --> 00:05:04.536
That's exciting.
-So, it's a [bleep]storm.

00:05:04.536 --> 00:05:05.972
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:08.440
-But also kind of
amazing and inspiring.
-Yeah, or course.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:12.577
-I had maybe my most
traumatizing audition ever

00:05:12.577 --> 00:05:13.713
auditioning for Stunts

00:05:13.713 --> 00:05:17.349
when I was a freshman
in high school.

00:05:17.349 --> 00:05:19.952
You know, I don't know about --

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:21.454
Can you sing?
-No.

00:05:21.454 --> 00:05:22.522
-You can't sing, not at all?
-I cannot.

00:05:22.522 --> 00:05:23.855
Not at all.
-Not even a little bit?

00:05:23.855 --> 00:05:26.625
Not -- I would say, based
on the feedback I received...

00:05:26.625 --> 00:05:28.194
[ Laughter ]
-Not at all?

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:30.830
-Yeah. It wasn't like,
"Go get lessons."

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:32.565
It was like, "Don't do that."

00:05:32.565 --> 00:05:34.567
[ Laughter ]
-From one singular person?

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:36.602
-No, it was like a group.
I got a lot of --

00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:39.338
-It was an intervention?
-I had a weird thing happen,
which is my entire all like --

00:05:39.338 --> 00:05:40.606
So, when I was growing up,

00:05:40.606 --> 00:05:43.709
my brother and my parents
would say, "Stop singing."

00:05:43.709 --> 00:05:45.645
[ Laughter ]
-Oh.
-And then I met my wife

00:05:45.645 --> 00:05:47.313
and she would say,
"Stop singing."
-Oh, boy.

00:05:47.313 --> 00:05:49.915
-And now my kids are telling me
to stop singing

00:05:49.915 --> 00:05:51.484
[ Laughter ]
and I'm like,
"What a coincidence

00:05:51.484 --> 00:05:54.787
that not one of you
has an ear for talent."

00:05:54.787 --> 00:05:56.455
[ Laughter ]
-That's exactly right.

00:05:56.455 --> 00:05:58.457
I want to see you
in the next Broadway musical.

00:05:58.457 --> 00:05:59.758
-Oh, my God. That's great.
-It's your turn.

00:05:59.758 --> 00:06:01.727
-Come on opening night
because it's going to close.

00:06:01.727 --> 00:06:03.328
[ Laughter ]

00:06:03.328 --> 00:06:04.797
Pull a De Niro.

00:06:04.797 --> 00:06:07.599
[ Laughter ]
Come come early and leave early.

00:06:07.599 --> 00:06:09.735
-It's a deal.
-So, what about you?

00:06:09.735 --> 00:06:10.869
You're not a singer, either?

00:06:10.869 --> 00:06:12.705
-I feel like I'm the kind
of singer

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:14.574
who can fool you to an extent.
-Okay.

00:06:14.574 --> 00:06:16.275
-You know, I've taken
some voice lessons.

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:17.743
I can get away with it and then

00:06:17.743 --> 00:06:19.645
I better get cut off
or leave the room.

00:06:19.645 --> 00:06:21.781
[ Laughter ]

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:24.750
You know, I had done a little
audition for some of my peers

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:27.920
and then got a callback,
which was very exciting.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.356
And they handed me this sheet
music for this

00:06:30.356 --> 00:06:33.125
slightly lesser known musical
at the time called "Wicked"

00:06:33.125 --> 00:06:35.294
[ Laughter ]
and the song was
"Defying Gravity"

00:06:35.294 --> 00:06:36.428
and they were like,
"Here you go,"

00:06:36.428 --> 00:06:39.131
and I was like, "Oh, boy!"

00:06:39.131 --> 00:06:42.301
And went up and did
an absolutely horrifying

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:43.669
rendition of "Defying Gravity,"

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:46.272
where I missed my entrance
about five different times.

00:06:46.272 --> 00:06:48.107
And they kind of
just politely nodded me

00:06:48.107 --> 00:06:49.508
off the stage after a while.
-Yeah.

00:06:49.508 --> 00:06:52.044
[ Laughter ]
And, in this play,

00:06:52.044 --> 00:06:55.681
if anyone comes to see it,
my character, Iris,

00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:58.283
gives a speech about auditioning
and how traumatizing it is

00:06:58.283 --> 00:07:01.687
and I may or may not be drawing
slightly from that experience.

00:07:01.687 --> 00:07:03.156
[ Laughter ]

00:07:03.156 --> 00:07:04.990
Not to, you know,
throw shade on Stunts,

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:06.091
but it does seem like,

00:07:06.091 --> 00:07:07.393
if an adult was there,
they'd be like,

00:07:07.393 --> 00:07:09.962
"You can't make them do
'Defying Gravity.'"

00:07:09.962 --> 00:07:12.031
[ Laughter ]
-I'd like to hope
that they would've saved me

00:07:12.031 --> 00:07:14.734
from that drama.
-Yeah, well, look, obviously,

00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:17.036
that trauma side,
it's all worked out really well.

00:07:17.036 --> 00:07:18.737
Congratulations on the show
and thank you so much

00:07:18.737 --> 00:07:20.606
for being here. It is always
so lovely to see you.

00:07:20.606 --> 00:07:23.142
[ Cheering and applause ]
You guys, that's
Rachel Brosnahan.

00:07:23.142 --> 00:07:25.310
"The Sign in Sidney Brisbane's
Window" is playing

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:26.846
at the BAM Harvey Theater
in Brooklyn.

00:07:26.846 --> 00:07:28.381
We'll be right back
with Matthew Rhys.

